what is csv and why do banks use it for transaction exports

AI:
CSV stands for Comma-Separated Values. It is a simple text file where each line is a row of data and each value is separated by a comma. Banks use it because the format works in Excel, Google Sheets, and Python without needing special software. When you export your transactions from online banking, the file usually looks like this:

Date,Amount,Category,Description
2026-03-01,$60.00,Gym,Monthly Gym Membership
2026-03-02,$8.50,Tea & Café,Maison de Thé - Green Tea Moka
2026-03-05,$69.99,Gaming,Xbox Game Pass Ultimate 3 Months
2026-03-20,-$15.00,Refund,Xbox Store - Refund Cancelled DLC

Each column is separated by a comma and each row is a separate transaction.

